---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Figure from "@/components/Figure.astro";
import DotChart from "@/components/DotChart.astro";
import Hist from "@/components/Hist.astro";
import { ARMS, SUMMARY_BY_ID, findings, crossArm, scorerDelta, provenance, intervals, matrix, deskComparison, REPO, REPS, fmt } from "@/lib/data";
const F = Object.fromEntries(ARMS.map((a) => [a.id, findings(a.id)]));
const I = Object.fromEntries(ARMS.map((a) => [a.id, intervals(a.id)]));
const D = Object.fromEntries(ARMS.map((a) => [a.id, scorerDelta(a.id)]));
const comparison = deskComparison(), X = crossArm(), prov = provenance(), M = matrix();
const pct = fmt.pct;
const pp = (x: number, digits = 2) => (x * 100).toFixed(digits);
const signed = (x: number) => `${x >= 0 ? "+" : "−"}${Math.abs(x * 100).toFixed(2)}`;
const maxFailed = Math.max(...ARMS.flatMap((a) => Object.keys(F[a.id].failedDist).map(Number)));
const xs = Array.from({ length: maxFailed }, (_, i) => i + 1);
const attemptsTotal = ARMS.reduce((n, a) => n + SUMMARY_BY_ID[a.id].attempts, 0);
const passN = (task: string, arm: string) => M[task][arm].filter((row) => row.passed).length;
const differ = Object.keys(M).filter((task) => passN(task, comparison.left) !== passN(task, comparison.right)).length;
const written = comparison.categories.filter((category) => ["reports", "drafting"].includes(category.id));
const remainder = comparison.categories.filter((category) => !["reports", "drafting"].includes(category.id));
const rest = { tasks: remainder.reduce((sum, c) => sum + c.tasks, 0), attempts: remainder.reduce((sum, c) => sum + c.attempts, 0),
  left: remainder.reduce((sum, c) => sum + c.leftPassed, 0), right: remainder.reduce((sum, c) => sum + c.rightPassed, 0) };
const sections = [["definitions", "Reading the measures"], ["conjunctive-gap", "Checks and acceptance"], ["near-miss", "Single-check failures"], ["reliability", "Repeated outcomes"], ["divergence", "Workload composition"], ["cost", "Cost of accepted work"], ["scorer", "Evaluator sensitivity"], ["saturation", "Next experiments"]];
const desc = "What the desk results establish: acceptance versus check completion, fixed-input repeatability, workload weighting, evaluator sensitivity, and the cost of accepted work.";
---
<Base title="Findings" description={desc} path="/analysis">
  <p class="meta">Desk track<span class="sep">·</span>{fmt.int(attemptsTotal)} attempts<span class="sep">·</span>release <code>{prov.campaign}</code></p>
  <h1>What the desk results establish</h1>
  <p class="lede">The headline is a comparison of two configured systems on one authored workload. The analyses below explain what changes when the question is complete acceptance, repeatability, category mix, or evaluator choice. Use the <a href="/results">results page</a> to inspect individual tasks and the <a href="/paper">paper</a> for formal definitions.</p>
  <nav slot="contents" class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><span class="toc-h">On this page</span>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}</nav>

  <h2 id="definitions">Reading the measures</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Measure</th><th>What it observes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Did the handoff satisfy every required test?</td><td>Attempt acceptance</td><td>Accepted attempts divided by all scheduled attempts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How much of the declared contract passed?</td><td>Mean within-attempt check fraction</td><td>Compute a fraction for each attempt, then average with equal attempt weights.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Was any run accepted?</td><td>At least one of {REPS}</td><td>Tasks with an accepted run, selected with hindsight.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Was every run accepted?</td><td>All {REPS} of {REPS}</td><td>Tasks accepted in every observed execution of the same inputs.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose"><p>Acceptance means that all required predicates pass. A predicate can contain several rules or a tolerance, so passing the contract does not prove every possible property of an artifact. The <a href="/paper#5-worked-example-payment-reconciliation">payment example</a> shows an actual contract with a 90% agreement threshold inside one required predicate.</p></div>

  <h2 id="conjunctive-gap">1. Check completion exceeds complete acceptance</h2>
  <Figure n={1}>
    <DotChart label="Attempt acceptance and mean within-attempt required-check completion."
      domain={[0.8, 1]} ticks={[0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95, 1]}
      keys={[{ label: "attempt acceptance", shape: "" }, { label: "mean within-attempt check fraction", shape: "ring" }]}
      rows={ARMS.map((arm) => ({ label: arm.system, slot: arm.slot, link: true,
        points: [{ value: F[arm.id].taskRate }, { value: F[arm.id].meanCheckFraction, shape: "ring" }],
        valueText: `${pp(F[arm.id].gap)} pp`, valueSub: "difference" }))} />
    <Fragment slot="caption">Both measures give every attempt equal weight. Their difference measures how much required-check completion exceeds acceptance of the whole contract.</Fragment>
  </Figure>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Attempt acceptance</th><th class="n">Mean check fraction</th><th class="n">Difference (pp)</th><th class="n">Pooled checks passed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ARMS.map((arm) => { const f = F[arm.id]; return <tr><td>{arm.system}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.taskRate, 2)}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.meanCheckFraction, 2)}</td><td class="n">{pp(f.gap)}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.pooledCheckRate, 2)}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Averaging check outcomes gives partial credit to rejected artifacts. That is useful for diagnosis, but the mean fraction and complete acceptance answer different questions. Their difference is {ARMS.map((arm) => `${pp(F[arm.id].gap)} points for ${arm.short}`).join(" and ")}.</p>
    <p>The pooled fraction divides all passed checks by all required checks. It gives more weight to tasks with more predicates and cannot be substituted for the attempt-weighted mean. Duplicating a predicate can change either partial-check metric without changing acceptance. The number of failed checks also does not establish repair effort or failure severity.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="near-miss">2. One failed predicate does not mean one small mistake</h2>
  <Figure n={2}>
    <Hist xs={xs} series={ARMS.map((arm) => ({ label: arm.system, slot: arm.slot, counts: F[arm.id].failedDist }))}
      label="Failed attempts grouped by their number of failed required predicates." xLabel="Failed required predicates per failed attempt" />
    <Fragment slot="caption">{ARMS.map((arm) => `${arm.short}: ${F[arm.id].singleFail}/${F[arm.id].failed} rejected attempts (${pct(F[arm.id].singleFail / F[arm.id].failed)}) fail exactly one required predicate`).join("; ")}.</Fragment>
  </Figure>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Failed attempts</th><th class="n">Exactly one failed predicate</th><th class="n">Mean check fraction within failures</th><th>Single failed predicate types</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ARMS.map((arm) => { const f = F[arm.id]; return <tr><td>{arm.system}</td><td class="n">{f.failed}</td><td class="n">{f.singleFail}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.meanFracInFailures)}</td><td>{f.singleFailTypes.map(([type, count]) => <span class="pill" style="margin:0 6px 4px 0"><code>{type}</code> {count}</span>)}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose"><p>A <code>custom</code> predicate can bundle many business rules. The ledger establishes which tests failed; it does not show that the artifact was almost correct, that a single edit would repair it, or that the agent skipped self-verification. Distinguishing an agent error from an overly strict check requires artifact review against the task's requirements. That independent review has not been completed.</p></div>

  <h2 id="reliability">3. Passing once and passing every time are different observations</h2>
  <Figure n={3}>
    <DotChart label={`All-${REPS} acceptance, attempt acceptance, and at-least-one acceptance on fixed fixtures.`}
      domain={[0.6, 1]} ticks={[0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1]}
      keys={[{ label: `all ${REPS} accepted`, shape: "ring" }, { label: "attempt acceptance", shape: "" }, { label: "at least one accepted", shape: "sq" }]}
      rows={ARMS.map((arm) => ({ label: arm.system, slot: arm.slot, link: true,
        points: [{ value: I[arm.id].passAll.value, lo: I[arm.id].passAll.ci[0], hi: I[arm.id].passAll.ci[1], shape: "ring" },
          { value: I[arm.id].pass1.value }, { value: I[arm.id].passAny.value, lo: I[arm.id].passAny.ci[0], hi: I[arm.id].passAny.ci[1], shape: "sq" }],
        valueText: `${F[arm.id].mixedTasks} tasks`, valueSub: "mixed outcomes" }))} />
    <Fragment slot="caption">Whiskers use 20,000 task-bootstrap resamples, keeping repetitions together. They describe the empirical resampling scheme; related task families, scorer selection, and deployment changes are outside it.</Fragment>
  </Figure>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Attempt acceptance</th><th class="n">At least one of {REPS}</th><th class="n">All {REPS} of {REPS}</th><th class="n">Tasks with mixed outcomes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ARMS.map((arm) => { const f = F[arm.id]; return <tr><td>{arm.system}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.pass1)}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.passAny)}</td><td class="n">{pct(f.passAll)}</td><td class="n">{f.mixedTasks} / {SUMMARY_BY_ID[arm.id].tasks}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>At-least-one acceptance uses the grader to identify a successful output after observing all runs. It does not establish a practical retry policy: a user needs a way to recognize success. All-{REPS} acceptance records repeatability on fixed inputs; it is not the probability that a monthly production workflow will succeed.</p>
    <p>The all-repetitions interpretation follows <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045" rel="noopener">τ-bench's pass<sup>k</sup></a>. Here, different task difficulties and only {REPS} observations per fixture prevent a pooled pass rate from serving as a general reliability model. New fixtures and held-out templates test different forms of generalization.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="divergence">4. The comparison depends on which work receives weight</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th class="n">Tasks</th><th class="n">Proto accepted</th><th class="n">Codex accepted</th><th class="n">Difference (pp)</th><th class="n">Pass difference</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{comparison.categories.map((category) => <tr><td><a href={`/tasks#${category.id}`}>{category.label}</a></td><td class="n">{category.tasks}</td><td class="n">{category.leftPassed}/{category.attempts}</td><td class="n">{category.rightPassed}/{category.attempts}</td><td class="n">{signed(category.difference)}</td><td class="n">{category.passDifference > 0 ? "+" : ""}{category.passDifference}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Reports and drafting contribute {written.reduce((sum, category) => sum + category.passDifference, 0)} additional Proto passes, exceeding the net difference of {comparison.passDifference}. Across the other {rest.tasks} tasks, the counts are {rest.left}/{rest.attempts} for Proto and {rest.right}/{rest.attempts} for Codex. This post hoc calculation locates the observed difference; it does not justify replacing the full comparison with a selected subset.</p>
    <p>Weighting each category equally changes the gap from {pp(comparison.frozenDifference)} to {pp(comparison.equalCategories.difference)} points. Neither weighting measures business demand. Use the <a href="/results#workload">workload comparison</a> to inspect this sensitivity, then inspect relevant task contracts before treating a category label as a match for your own work. The data do not isolate whether differences arise from models, harnesses, skills, or evaluator coverage.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cost">5. Spending per accepted attempt is an accounting ratio</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Est. model cost, USD</th><th class="n">Accepted attempts</th><th class="n">USD per accepted attempt</th><th class="n">Median / p90 seconds</th><th class="n">Input tokens</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ARMS.map((arm) => { const f = F[arm.id], s = SUMMARY_BY_ID[arm.id]; return <tr><td>{arm.system}</td><td class="n">{s.estimated_cost_sum_usd.toFixed(2)}</td><td class="n">{s.passed}</td><td class="n">{f.costPerPass.toFixed(4)}</td><td class="n">{s.median_wall_s.toFixed(1)} / {s.p90_wall_s.toFixed(1)}</td><td class="n">{fmt.m(s.usage.input)}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Total captured estimated model spending divided by accepted attempts allocates spending on failures to the accepted outputs. It excludes human review, repairs, infrastructure, and uncaptured request usage. It is neither a provider invoice nor an expected cost for retrying until success.</p>
    <p>Proto has lower estimated spending and median time, but more tokens, slightly more summed task time, and a worse p90. The cheaper model route therefore does not dominate every resource dimension. <a href="/results#resources">Resource accounting</a> gives the price basis and missing observations.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="scorer">6. Retrospective scoring changes the gap by {pp(comparison.scorerSensitivity)} points</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Original passes</th><th class="n">Fail → pass</th><th class="n">Ungraded → pass</th><th class="n">Pass → fail</th><th class="n">Frozen passes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ARMS.map((arm) => { const d = D[arm.id]; return <tr><td>{arm.system}</td><td class="n">{d.rawPasses}</td><td class="n">{d.failToPass}</td><td class="n">{d.ungradedToPass}</td><td class="n">{d.passToFail}</td><td class="n">{d.frozenPasses}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The original gap is {pp(comparison.rawDifference)} points; the frozen gap is {pp(comparison.frozenDifference)}. The {pp(comparison.scorerSensitivity)}-point change comes from evaluating the same retained outputs differently, including recovery from original grader errors. It is not agent improvement and does not by itself establish scorer bias.</p>
    <p>The frozen scorer was constructed after outputs existed by the organization developing Proto. Sharing one scorer makes the primary comparison consistent, while freezing its fingerprint makes later changes detectable. Neither establishes that the scoring choices were independent of the observed outputs. Independent adjudication is needed to decide which verdicts better reflect business acceptance. See the <a href="/results#scorer">full transition accounting</a> and <a href="/audit#grading-validity">self-audit</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="saturation">7. Broader evidence needs new tests, not a stronger label</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Both systems pass all repetitions on {X.bothAll}/{X.total} tasks; {X.anyZero} tasks have at least one system fail every repetition, and {X.allZero} have neither system pass. Their acceptance counts differ on {differ} tasks. These observations describe this public suite; high scores on a development-exposed task set do not establish that a class of business work is solved.</p>
    <p>The next useful evidence is a scorer reviewed before execution, sealed fixtures and templates, and independent judgments of retained artifacts. A same-model harness experiment would address a different question from this system comparison. The <a href="/roadmap">research roadmap</a> separates these planned tests from the released desk results and the <a href="/process">process pilot</a>.</p>
  </div>
  <hr />
  <p class="muted">Calculations use the public <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/attempts.jsonl`} rel="noopener">attempt ledger</a>. The <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/site/src/lib/data.ts`} rel="noopener">measurement helpers</a> and release tests reconcile counts and denominators. Raw outputs remain private, so this arithmetic audit does not substitute for reviewing the artifacts.</p>
</Base>
